    "https://nsuok.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/5941?c=nsuok": {
      "title": "F99451 Assistant Professor of Leadership",
      "description": "F99451 Assistant Professor of Leadership | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) F99451 Assistant Professor of Leadership Tahlequah, OK, United States | req5941 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/28/2026 Back to Search F99451 Assistant Professor of Leadership Position Summary The Department of Educational Leadership at Northeastern State University invites applications for a full-time faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor. This position is Oklahoma-based in Tahlequah, OK. The successful candidate will support graduate education through high-quality teaching, an active program of research and scholarship, meaningful university and professional service, and sustained mentorship of graduate students in the Master of Science in Leadership and Doctor of Education (Ed.D.) in Community-Based Leadership programs. The Ed.D. in Community-Based Leadership is an innovative, cohort-based doctoral program designed to prepare scholar-practitioners who create sustainable change through collaboration among educational institutions, tribal nations, nonprofit organizations, government agencies, and community partners. Through applied research and a Dissertation in Practice, doctoral students address authentic leadership challenges while developing evidence-based solutions that strengthen organizations and communities. This position offers an exceptional opportunity for an accomplished scholar-practitioner to help shape and grow a distinctive doctoral program. The successful candidate will play a significant role in curriculum development, doctoral student success, research initiatives, community partnerships, and the continued advancement of graduate education. Faculty members are expected to contribute to a collaborative academic culture that values innovation, applied scholarship, mentorship, and community engagement. Why Join the Department of Educational Leadership? The Department of Educational Leadership is committed to preparing ethical, innovative, and community-engaged leaders through graduate education grounded in research, collaboration, and professional practice. Faculty enjoy a collegial environment characterized by shared governance, interdisciplinary collaboration, and a strong commitment to student success. Faculty have opportunities to: Help shape a growing doctoral program with a distinctive mission. Mentor highly motivated master's and doctoral students in a cohort-based learning environment. Collaborate with faculty across disciplines on research, teaching, and community-engaged scholarship. Build partnerships with PK–12 schools, higher education institutions, tribal nations, nonprofit organizations, businesses, and governmental agencies. Develop externally funded research initiatives and interdisciplinary projects. Assume leadership roles in doctoral education, curriculum development, assessment, accreditation, and program innovation. Contribute to a department that values academic excellence, collaborative leadership, and meaningful community impact. Desired Characteristics The ideal candidate is a collaborative scholar-practitioner who is passionate about mentoring doctoral students, advancing applied and community-engaged research, and preparing ethical leaders capable of transforming organizations and communities. Successful candidates will demonstrate a commitment to innovation, interdisciplinary collaboration, inclusive excellence, and integrating scholarship with professional practice. They will embrace opportunities to build a nationally recognized doctoral program that prepares leaders to address complex educational and societal challenges through research-informed action. Primary Responsibilities - Graduate Teaching This position is intended for candidates with a broad foundation in organizational leadership and the ability to apply leadership theory, research, and practice across diverse organizational and community contexts. The department seeks a versatile scholar-practitioner whose expertise extends beyond a single sector or leadership specialization and who can engage with leadership challenges across educational institutions, nonprofit organizations, tribal nations, governmental agencies, businesses, and community-based organizations. Candidates should demonstrate the capacity to teach across multiple areas of leadership, mentor graduate students with varied professional backgrounds, and connect organizational leadership principles to complex, real-world challenges. Faculty responsibilities include a balanced combination of graduate teaching, scholarly research, doctoral mentoring, and professional service consistent with the mission of a comprehensive regional university. Responsibilities include: Teach graduate courses in the M.S. in Leadership and Ed.D. in Community-Based Leadership programs. Teach doctoral-level research courses in qualitative, quantitative, mixed methods, improvement science, practitioner inquiry, and Dissertation in Practice. Teach courses in leadership theory, organizational leadership, educational leadership, strategic leadership, ethics, policy, organizational change, and community-based leadership. Develop engaging online and blended graduate courses using Blackboard Ultra. Employ learner-centered instructional strategies that foster engagement, collaboration, and scholarly inquiry. Participate in curriculum development and continuous program improvement. Provide timely, substantive feedback that supports graduate student success. Doctoral Mentorship Chair doctoral dissertation committees from prospectus through final defense. Serve on dissertation and comprehensive examination committees. Mentor doctoral students in research design, scholarly writing, data analysis, and ethical research practices. Guide students through qualitative, quantitative, and mixed methods research. Foster collaborative learning communities within doctoral cohorts. Support students in presenting and publishing scholarly work. Scholarship The department values an active scholarship agenda that advances leadership practice and contributes to meaningful educational and community change. Faculty are expected to: Maintain an active research agenda. Publish in peer-reviewed journals and scholarly books. Present research at state, national, and international conferences. Pursue external funding and collaborative grant opportunities. Engage in interdisciplinary and community-engaged scholarship. Produce research that informs leadership practice, organizational improvement, and community development. Service and Leadership Faculty members are expected to contribute to the continuous improvement of graduate education through institutional leadership and professional service. Responsibilities include: Participate in departmental, college, and university committees. Assist with doctoral admissions, interviews, recruitment, and student retention. Contribute to accreditation, assessment, and strategic planning initiatives. Develop partnerships with schools, community organizations, tribal nations, nonprofit organizations, and governmental agencies. Provide leadership within professional organizations. Serve in departmental leadership roles such as curriculum development, doctoral program coordination, dissertation coordination, assessment, or graduate faculty leadership. Required Qualifications Earned doctorate (Ph.D. or Ed.D.) in Educational Leadership, Leadership Studies, Higher Education, Organizational Leadership, Educational Research, or a closely related discipline from an accredited institution. Evidence of successful graduate-level teaching. Demonstrated expertise in qualitative and quantitative research methodologies. Experience teaching graduate research methods and doctoral research courses. Experience chairing or serving on doctoral dissertation committees. Demonstrated success mentoring graduate and doctoral students. Experience teaching fully online and blended graduate courses. Demonstrated proficiency with Blackboard Ultra or comparable learning management systems. Record of scholarly productivity appropriate for appointment at the Assistant Professor rank. Excellent written, oral, and interpersonal communication skills. Preferred Qualifications Preference will be given to candidates with one or more of the following: Experience teaching in an Ed.D. or Ph.D. program. Expertise in community-based research, participatory action research, improvement science, implementation science, or practitioner inquiry. Experience with Dissertation in Practice models. Expertise in mixed methods research. Experience using statistical and qualitative analysis software (SPSS, R, NVivo, MAXQDA, or ATLAS.ti). Experience with cohort-based doctoral education. Record of successful grant writing and externally funded research. Experience with accreditation, program assessment, and continuous improvement. Experience integrating artificial intelligence, learning analytics, or emerging educational technologies into graduate teaching and research. Demonstrated experience developing partnerships with educational institutions, tribal nations, nonprofit organizations, governmental agencies, or community organizations. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities The successful candidate will demonstrate the ability to: Inspire and mentor graduate students as emerging scholar-practitioners. Design rigorous, engaging graduate learning experiences in online and blended environments. Guide doctoral students through every stage of the dissertation process. Teach advanced research methodology with clarity and rigor. Foster collaborative, interdisciplinary research. Build meaningful community partnerships that support applied scholarship. Balance teaching excellence with sustained scholarly productivity. Work collaboratively within a collegial, student-centered department. Advance the department's mission through innovation, service, and academic leadership. Areas of Teaching Expertise Preferred teaching expertise includes one or more of the following: Leadership Theory Organizational Leadership Community-Based Leadership Organizational Change Higher Education Leadership Qualitative Research Methods Quantitative Research Methods Mixed Methods Research Practitioner Inquiry Dissertation in Practice Research Design Ethics and Educational Policy Application Materials Applicants should submit: Letter of interest addressing qualifications and alignment with the position. Curriculum vitae. Statement of teaching philosophy. Statement of research agenda. Unofficial graduate transcripts (official transcripts required upon hire). Contact information for three professional references. Salary is $61,070 with excellent benefits The projected hire date is January 4, 2027 Applications are accepted until November 1, 2026 NOTE: Posting will close to applicants at 11:59 p.m. on the date above. Department Information for Applicants Faculty teaching may include distance-based courses, including blended and fully online classes. Transcripts may be scanned, saved, and uploaded. An official/certified transcript is required upon employment. Please include on the application complete contact information for three references. IMPORTANT APPLICANT INFORMATION FROM HUMAN RESOURCES Northeastern State University is an Oklahoma State agency. In accordance with such, any applicant hired for this position must provide a Social Security card to Human Resources during the hiring process. Northeastern State University complies with Section 503 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and is committed to equal employment opportunity. Apply Now Share Save Job Northeastern State University complies with all “applicable” federal and state laws regarding non-discrimination."
    },
